ideally put it in all caps

00:08:43.845 --> 00:08:46.271
so it's easier to see
'cause the chat goes quickly

00:08:46.271 --> 00:08:48.529
but first up is Lorraine.

00:08:48.529 --> 00:08:52.271
Lorraine says hi I'm
Lorraine from South Carolina,

00:08:52.271 --> 00:08:55.272
I'm stuck I'm hopin' that
you can help me get unstuck,

00:08:55.272 --> 00:08:58.162
my career, my livin' arrangements
all need an overhaul,

00:08:58.162 --> 00:08:59.950
I'm so overwhelmed right now,

00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:02.861
how do I start thank you, Lorraine.

00:09:02.861 --> 00:09:03.970
- Hey Lorraine.

00:09:03.970 --> 00:09:06.918
And then I'm going to ask Evan
a question at the end as well

00:09:06.918 --> 00:09:08.432
'cause he's going to answer your question

00:09:08.432 --> 00:09:10.765
as well really articulately.

00:09:12.616 --> 00:09:16.636
Welcome we love the Carolinas
by the way fantastic.

00:09:16.636 --> 00:09:20.542
I think when you feel
overwhelmed, you feel stuck.

00:09:20.542 --> 00:09:22.755
I think you kind of in
this moment Lorraine,

00:09:22.755 --> 00:09:25.597
it's really probably cool to just create

00:09:25.597 --> 00:09:27.557
a bit of a reflective space

00:09:27.557 --> 00:09:29.360
and come back to think of

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:33.527
what is your big idea going to
solve and who is the audience?

00:09:35.157 --> 00:09:37.343
What is their pain point
and how can you help

00:09:37.343 --> 00:09:39.905
them overcome that pain point.

00:09:39.905 --> 00:09:41.271
A beauty about this particularly

00:09:41.271 --> 00:09:42.573
when you become overwhelmed

00:09:42.573 --> 00:09:44.956
and you got a to-do list this long

00:09:44.956 --> 00:09:48.746
is cut back to the essence of your idea,

00:09:48.746 --> 00:09:50.435
your product, your service,

00:09:50.435 --> 00:09:52.009
why it's going to change the world

00:09:52.009 --> 00:09:53.499
and then the other thing,

00:09:53.499 --> 00:09:55.765
you know the magic of simplification,

00:09:55.765 --> 00:09:58.409
what does it do that you can actually

00:09:58.409 --> 00:10:02.135
in a consumer product make
the consumers like better

00:10:02.135 --> 00:10:04.438
or in a b to b sort of world,

00:10:04.438 --> 00:10:07.901
really try to take cost down
or improve their top line

00:10:07.901 --> 00:10:10.717
or take hassle out of their organization

00:10:10.717 --> 00:10:12.774
and less stress.

00:10:12.774 --> 00:10:15.008
So I think you just got
to come back to basics,

00:10:15.008 --> 00:10:17.692
just focus on where's your audience,

00:10:17.692 --> 00:10:21.045
what idea is going to
really change the world

00:10:21.045 --> 00:10:24.441
and create a market for
your product or service.

00:10:24.441 --> 00:10:26.959
And then if you do that,
you can get really clear

00:10:26.959 --> 00:10:29.030
about what's important.

00:10:29.030 --> 00:10:31.384
And actually one other thing,

00:10:31.384 --> 00:10:35.160
I hope I'm not doing
motherhood and apple pie here.

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:38.767
Loads of people get into this
is urgent, this is urgent,

00:10:38.767 --> 00:10:41.226
this is urgent and their
to-do list grows and grows

00:10:41.226 --> 00:10:44.668
and it becomes overwhelming
and your head's spinnin',

00:10:44.668 --> 00:10:47.854
I'd ask yourself if you've got ambition

00:10:47.854 --> 00:10:49.993
what's really important?

00:10:49.993 --> 00:10:52.910
'Cause everything can appear urgent

00:10:53.799 --> 00:10:56.444
but there's very few things
that are really important

00:10:56.444 --> 00:10:59.459
and what's going to really move the dial.

00:10:59.459 --> 00:11:02.632
And then just focus your
urges about movin' the dial

00:11:02.632 --> 00:11:04.685
and actually Lorraine
what you can do mentally

00:11:04.685 --> 00:11:06.471
is create a bucket in the corner

00:11:06.471 --> 00:11:08.520
and if there's lots of good ideas

00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:10.759
and they're not fundamentally important

00:11:10.759 --> 00:11:12.858
just write 'em down
chuck it in the bucket,

00:11:12.858 --> 00:11:15.046
come back to 'em in 90 days time.

00:11:15.046 --> 00:11:18.125
Really focus on what's important,

00:11:18.125 --> 00:11:20.992
I'd say where you get confidence from

00:11:20.992 --> 00:11:23.609
is not only the idea and then the strategy

00:11:23.609 --> 00:11:25.761
but where you get success from

00:11:25.761 --> 00:11:27.431
is awesome execution.

00:11:27.431 --> 00:11:29.859
Really executin' the hell out of an idea.

00:11:29.859 --> 00:11:31.729
So Evan question for you,

00:11:31.729 --> 00:11:34.167
obviously Lorraine's question but also,

00:11:34.167 --> 00:11:36.623
your inspiration I've met you in Toronto,

00:11:36.623 --> 00:11:39.636
I've met you around the world,

00:11:39.636 --> 00:11:42.469
where's your source of confidence?

00:11:44.029 --> 00:11:46.539
- I think two things for
my source of confidence,

00:11:46.539 --> 00:11:49.081
I think one is having,

00:11:49.081 --> 00:11:50.164
trying stuff,

00:11:51.309 --> 00:11:54.240
whenever I feel like I'm
afraid to do somethin'

00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:58.433
then I force myself to
have to go out and do it.

00:11:58.433 --> 00:12:02.588
Come and speak at this event,
here's a simple example,

00:12:02.588 --> 00:12:05.035
I'm walkin' down the
street, on the side street

00:12:05.035 --> 00:12:07.109
and I've got my ear buds on

00:12:07.109 --> 00:12:11.256
and I'm dancin' and I'm
puttin' on my favorite song

00:12:11.256 --> 00:12:12.453
and then I come to an intersection,

00:12:12.453 --> 00:12:15.470
it's a busy intersection,
then there's people,

00:12:15.470 --> 00:12:19.637
then my instinct is I got to
stop dancing, go really small

00:12:20.612 --> 00:12:23.939
because I'm worried about
how people might think of me

00:12:23.939 --> 00:12:25.985
and as soon as I caught that,

00:12:25.985 --> 00:12:27.891
I'm playing small right now
'cause I'm worried about

00:12:27.891 --> 00:12:29.217
what this person's going to think.

00:12:29.217 --> 00:12:31.538
Then I force myself to go out and do it

00:12:31.538 --> 00:12:35.008
and then I'm dancing big
on the street on purpose

00:12:35.008 --> 00:12:36.398
to stretch my muscle,

00:12:36.398 --> 00:12:39.186
so whenever you feel
yourself shrinking down

00:12:39.186 --> 00:12:40.031
if you can catch it,

00:12:40.031 --> 00:12:41.697
a lot of times it's so sub-conscious

00:12:41.697 --> 00:12:42.825
that you don't even catch it,

00:12:42.825 --> 00:12:45.121
if you can catch it then force yourself

00:12:45.121 --> 00:12:48.546
to step into that fear,
step into uncomfortness

00:12:48.546 --> 00:12:50.245
and start playin' a bigger game,

00:12:50.245 --> 00:12:53.933
the more you do that, the
more you'll get confidence,

00:12:53.933 --> 00:12:57.324
The other thing is just
havin' a better environment,

00:12:57.324 --> 00:12:59.206
I surround myself with entrepreneurs

00:12:59.206 --> 00:13:01.519
who've done great things.

00:13:01.519 --> 00:13:03.766
Whether you like quotes or books or videos

00:13:03.766 --> 00:13:06.799
or meetings, or going to conferences,

00:13:06.799 --> 00:13:09.686
surrounding yourself with
people doing bigger things,

00:13:09.686 --> 00:13:13.653
part of that seeps into you
and makes you more confident

00:13:13.653 --> 00:13:16.589
to go out and do your thing,

00:13:16.589 --> 00:13:19.279
I didn't have a ton of
confidence at the start.

00:13:19.279 --> 00:13:21.403
I didn't know what I was going to do,

00:13:21.403 --> 00:13:24.683
I didn't have entrepreneurs in my family,

00:13:24.683 --> 00:13:27.248
those two things forcing
the uncomfortableness,

00:13:27.248 --> 00:13:29.089
and then creating a better environment,

00:13:29.089 --> 00:13:31.636
gave me more confidence to move forward.

00:13:31.636 --> 00:13:33.881
- And what about thoughts
you could give to Lorraine,

00:13:33.881 --> 00:13:37.769
down the Carolinas, how could
she become less overwhelmed

00:13:37.769 --> 00:13:40.436
and really focus on her success?

00:13:41.441 --> 00:13:44.601
- I agree with you, I liked
a lot of what you said,

00:13:44.601 --> 00:13:46.420
I think you just got to simplify,

00:13:46.420 --> 00:13:48.807
I would look at what is the one thing,

00:13:48.807 --> 00:13:52.670
that if I focus on that, that
would really have a big impact

00:13:52.670 --> 00:13:54.066
and have a ripple effect.

00:13:54.066 --> 00:13:56.482
'Cause once you start
making progress on one thing

00:13:56.482 --> 00:13:57.963
and you're getting some results,

00:13:57.963 --> 00:14:00.709
it bleeds into everything
else you're doing.

00:14:00.709 --> 00:14:04.551
Once you're more confident
in a business deal,

00:14:04.551 --> 00:14:07.894
you become more confident in
your personal life as well,

00:14:07.894 --> 00:14:09.540
it bleeds through everything.

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:11.660
So I'd be looking at, okay
there's a thousand things you

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:14.373
have to do, what's the one
single most important thing

00:14:14.373 --> 00:14:16.856
that if you fix this,

00:14:16.856 --> 00:14:18.466
It'll not only help that one situation

00:14:18.466 --> 00:14:20.383
but everything around it as well.

00:14:20.383 --> 00:14:22.414
Then focus on doing that

00:14:22.414 --> 00:14:25.914
and eliminate the distractions around him.

00:14:27.014 --> 00:14:28.746
- That's interesting,
yeah I think actually

00:14:28.746 --> 00:14:30.745
just thinkin' about this Evan,

00:14:30.745 --> 00:14:32.570
there's an element here,

00:14:32.570 --> 00:14:35.523
I'm not a psychologist , I
would never pretend to be,

00:14:35.523 --> 00:14:38.449
but the psychology of success,

00:14:38.449 --> 00:14:42.333
Lorraine you know and I know
if you start off in the morning

00:14:42.333 --> 00:14:45.085
and you set yourself the toughest task,

00:14:45.085 --> 00:14:47.066
and you don't feel you
do a good job of it,

00:14:47.066 --> 00:14:49.644
it's going to determine your
mood and your confidence

00:14:49.644 --> 00:14:51.668
for the rest of the day.

00:14:51.668 --> 00:14:55.034
So maybe first thing you do is if you know

00:14:55.034 --> 00:14:56.995
you can smash it out of the park,

00:14:56.995 --> 00:15:00.428
and then you will feel 10 million dollars

00:15:00.428 --> 00:15:02.501
and you will be able to
crush the next thing,

00:15:02.501 --> 00:15:06.668
what Evan said, you can build
rolling thunder off success,

00:15:08.056 --> 00:15:10.313
we talk about actually in Canada they talk

00:15:10.313 --> 00:15:12.678
about gettin' the moose on
the table, cuttin' it up,

00:15:12.678 --> 00:15:15.074
in Europe gettin' the
elephant in the room,

00:15:15.074 --> 00:15:16.299
all this sort of stuff

00:15:16.299 --> 00:15:19.319
but if you can slice these
things down into areas

00:15:19.319 --> 00:15:21.456
where I know I can do that, smash it,

00:15:21.456 --> 00:15:24.162
know I can do that smash it out the park,

00:15:24.162 --> 00:15:27.955
you are unstoppable, you are relentless

00:15:27.955 --> 00:15:29.466
but give yourself things,

00:15:29.466 --> 00:15:32.596
treat yourself to some early successes,

00:15:32.596 --> 00:15:35.182
that will really build
your confidence honestly,

00:15:35.182 --> 00:15:36.651
'cause you and I know,

00:15:36.651 --> 00:15:39.158
if you start yourself and you
hit your head against the wall

00:15:39.158 --> 00:15:41.065
and you bounce off,

00:15:41.065 --> 00:15:42.739
it takes time to dust yourself off

00:15:42.739 --> 00:15:45.681
and go back and smash
against that wall again,

00:15:45.681 --> 00:15:47.497
but if you actually give
yourself some things

00:15:47.497 --> 00:15:50.643
on your list of priorities
that are really important,

00:15:50.643 --> 00:15:52.870
where you can deliver early success

00:15:52.870 --> 00:15:57.850
then that success becomes
insatiable and unstoppable.

00:15:57.850 --> 00:15:58.683
- I love it!

00:15:58.683 --> 00:16:00.149
Lorraine thank you for the question,

00:16:00.149 --> 00:16:02.430
lots of love good luck.

00:16:02.430 --> 00:16:04.794
- [Stephen] Good luck Lorraine.

00:16:04.794 --> 00:16:07.605
- Relaxing sleep station,

00:16:07.605 --> 00:16:10.868
I've started opening up
very publicly using my story

00:16:10.868 --> 00:16:13.651
as a differentiator, I'm still concerned

00:16:13.651 --> 00:16:15.279
that I'll need to get a job in the future

00:16:15.279 --> 00:16:17.547
and keep my business as my side hustle

00:16:17.547 --> 00:16:19.991
until it earns enough,

00:16:19.991 --> 00:16:22.604
how do I be true to my
story while not completely

00:16:22.604 --> 00:16:25.083
turning off potential
employers who will find

00:16:25.083 --> 00:16:29.582
all this information with me
with a simple google search?

00:16:29.582 --> 00:16:32.288
- I think it's great, I think actually

00:16:32.288 --> 00:16:35.890
and I talk about the authenticity,

00:16:35.890 --> 00:16:39.317
I'd say a lot of great start-ups

00:16:39.317 --> 00:16:42.807
are spawned out of people like yourself

00:16:42.807 --> 00:16:45.783
who work in doin' a full
time job for someone else

00:16:45.783 --> 00:16:47.832
and then in the evenings and weekends,

00:16:47.832 --> 00:16:49.577
creating their ideas

00:16:49.577 --> 00:16:51.826
and then ultimately building a business,

00:16:51.826 --> 00:16:55.970
it's great if you can get
a big corporation to pay

00:16:55.970 --> 00:16:58.609
for you to be moonlightin'

00:16:58.609 --> 00:17:02.154
and creating the next amazing business

00:17:02.154 --> 00:17:03.939
that you're going to create,

00:17:03.939 --> 00:17:06.176
in your bedroom, or in the garage,

00:17:06.176 --> 00:17:07.597
or wherever you're doin' it,

00:17:07.597 --> 00:17:09.046
then that's a fantastic thing to do,

00:17:09.046 --> 00:17:10.704
so I don't think there should

00:17:10.704 --> 00:17:12.614
be any guilt associated with that.

00:17:12.614 --> 00:17:16.513
I think that's the way
people operate in 2016,

00:17:16.513 --> 00:17:17.925
I think the other thing honestly,

00:17:17.925 --> 00:17:21.182
if people came to us in our company

00:17:21.182 --> 00:17:24.750
and said actually I want
to start a business,

00:17:24.750 --> 00:17:28.232
I'd applaud you, they can
be full time employees

00:17:28.232 --> 00:17:30.972
and colleagues with me in Sage

00:17:30.972 --> 00:17:33.355
and I think you will be surprised,

00:17:33.355 --> 00:17:35.678
there's a lot of entrepreneurs out there,

00:17:35.678 --> 00:17:39.198
even leaders of big companies out there

00:17:39.198 --> 00:17:41.265
who would really
encourage their colleagues

00:17:41.265 --> 00:17:44.598
and their employees to start their ideas

00:17:45.688 --> 00:17:47.206
and incubate their ideas

00:17:47.206 --> 00:17:49.373
and then get ready to fly,

00:17:50.833 --> 00:17:52.442
I think it's worth pursuing,

00:17:52.442 --> 00:17:56.375
when I just look at the
range of businesses,

00:17:56.375 --> 00:17:57.645
I'll give you an example,

00:17:57.645 --> 00:17:58.965
Obviously I've mentioned this guy before,

00:17:58.965 --> 00:18:02.678
he's a genius and that's
Marc Benioff at Salesforce,

00:18:02.678 --> 00:18:05.834
Marc Benioff's worked
with me bact at oracle

00:18:05.834 --> 00:18:08.124
and Larry Ellison the chief
executive and chairman

00:18:08.124 --> 00:18:09.598
at the time of Oracle,

00:18:09.598 --> 00:18:13.369
knew Mark was startin' his
business and was really happy

00:18:13.369 --> 00:18:15.458
for him to be incubatin' his business

00:18:15.458 --> 00:18:19.035
while he was moonlightin' and
workin' full time at Oracle

00:18:19.035 --> 00:18:22.327
and now Mark, the evaluation
of that company sells for

00:18:22.327 --> 00:18:24.568
must be 60 billion dollars,

00:18:24.568 --> 00:18:26.652
one of the greatest
companies on the planet,

00:18:26.652 --> 00:18:28.937
got huge respect for him
and he does amazing things

00:18:28.937 --> 00:18:32.109
around communities through
the Salesforce Foundation,

00:18:32.109 --> 00:18:34.338
giving back to communities,
making a difference

00:18:34.338 --> 00:18:38.505
and buildin' and really leadin'
the collaborary revolution.

00:18:39.397 --> 00:18:42.044
That's a real example and that happened

00:18:42.044 --> 00:18:43.902
what 16, 17 years ago,

00:18:43.902 --> 00:18:48.051
I think we shouldn't constrain
ourselves to just say,

00:18:48.051 --> 00:18:51.474
I work for a company, I
leave them and do a start-up,

00:18:51.474 --> 00:18:55.482
I think there could be
almost a perfect meltin' pot

00:18:55.482 --> 00:18:58.228
where you can be workin'
and workin' on your own idea

00:18:58.228 --> 00:19:00.041
at the same time and
startin' your business

00:19:00.041 --> 00:19:03.666
and maybe even getting buddies
to start the business for you

00:19:03.666 --> 00:19:07.099
and then at the right time
you kind of flip over.

00:19:07.099 --> 00:19:10.702
- I think this ties in
really well to this theme

00:19:10.702 --> 00:19:13.684
of havin' the confidence
to be your true self,

00:19:13.684 --> 00:19:17.267
because if you are
worried about a customer

00:19:18.947 --> 00:19:20.530
not buying from you because of your story

00:19:20.530 --> 00:19:22.637
or a company not hiring
you because of your story,

00:19:22.637 --> 00:19:26.087
that's probably not going to
be a great fit for you anyway,

00:19:26.087 --> 00:19:27.665
if you're looking for a part time job

00:19:27.665 --> 00:19:29.583
to support your business on the side,

00:19:29.583 --> 00:19:30.828
there's going to be
some companies who will

00:19:30.828 --> 00:19:33.786
look at what you're doing and
won't want to hire somebody

00:19:33.786 --> 00:19:36.451
who has their own part time
thing, they want to hire

00:19:36.451 --> 00:19:39.401
somebody who's only
dedicated to their company

00:19:39.401 --> 00:19:41.288
and there's going to be other people who,

00:19:41.288 --> 00:19:43.074
will look at that as an advantage,

00:19:43.074 --> 00:19:46.459
oh you're an entrepreneur,
that's great you can help us

00:19:46.459 --> 00:19:48.442
'cause you understand entrepreneurs

00:19:48.442 --> 00:19:49.689
and you could give us advice

00:19:49.689 --> 00:19:52.807
and do a great job in your role,

00:19:52.807 --> 00:19:54.839
by putting that content out there

00:19:54.839 --> 00:19:58.853
and by being yourself,
you'll attract the companies

00:19:58.853 --> 00:20:00.472
that are a better fit for you

00:20:00.472 --> 00:20:02.671
and the companies that aren't a great fit

00:20:02.671 --> 00:20:03.821
they won't be interested in you,

00:20:03.821 --> 00:20:05.014
it's the same thing with your customers,

00:20:05.014 --> 00:20:06.838
if you're sharing your story,

00:20:06.838 --> 00:20:09.127
there's some people
who won't connect to it

00:20:09.127 --> 00:20:12.459
and that's fine those guys
would not be great customers

00:20:12.459 --> 00:20:13.724
for you and there's going to be others

00:20:13.724 --> 00:20:16.352
who will madly, deeply resonate with it

00:20:16.352 --> 00:20:20.408
and drive that extra 100
miles just to get to you

00:20:20.408 --> 00:20:23.408
because they love what you're about,

00:20:24.244 --> 00:20:27.913
I hire people even if they have
their own thing on the side,

00:20:27.913 --> 00:20:30.435
I have 18 people on my team
it's not anywhere close

00:20:30.435 --> 00:20:31.896
to what Sage is at,

00:20:31.896 --> 00:20:34.222
but you just heard it
from the CEO there too,

00:20:34.222 --> 00:20:36.266
thousands and thousands of employees

00:20:36.266 --> 00:20:39.657
and they value people who have
things on the side as well,

00:20:39.657 --> 00:20:43.157
I would encourage you to go in and embrace

00:20:44.066 --> 00:20:45.825
what you're doin' and maybe
even include it on your resume

00:20:45.825 --> 00:20:48.492
as opposed to tryin' to hide it.

00:20:50.708 --> 00:20:52.108
- The other thing Evan the only area

00:20:52.108 --> 00:20:55.191
where it could get a bit testy is if,

00:20:56.395 --> 00:20:58.995
I think this speaks to
authenticity, integrity

00:20:58.995 --> 00:21:02.016
and your values, obviously
it wouldn't be appropriate

00:21:02.016 --> 00:21:05.636
to work for a company and
then steal their ideas

00:21:05.636 --> 00:21:07.644
to become a competitor of that company.

00:21:07.644 --> 00:21:08.803
- [Evan] Sure.

00:21:08.803 --> 00:21:11.173
- I think the confidence comes

00:21:11.173 --> 00:21:13.810
through having really powerful values

00:21:13.810 --> 00:21:16.056
and part of that is integrity

00:21:16.056 --> 00:21:18.992
and also connectin'
yourself to the customer

00:21:18.992 --> 00:21:22.590
but I think as long as it's not a conflict

00:21:22.590 --> 00:21:25.513
and you're not going to rip off

00:21:25.513 --> 00:21:28.646
the company's intellectual
property or stuff like that

00:21:28.646 --> 00:21:31.499
then I think there's a
lot of companies out there

00:21:31.499 --> 00:21:33.693
where exactly to Evan's point,

00:21:33.693 --> 00:21:36.679
you can find a perfect
marriage with you and a company

00:21:36.679 --> 00:21:39.408
and their ambition for their colleagues

00:21:39.408 --> 00:21:41.908
to be really happy, fulfilled,

00:21:45.276 --> 00:21:47.945
I do believe companies of the future,

00:21:47.945 --> 00:21:50.451
should believe they have
a relationship for life

00:21:50.451 --> 00:21:52.578
with the colleagues who work for them

00:21:52.578 --> 00:21:55.618
and that might mean I'd
say to these people here,

00:21:55.618 --> 00:21:57.060
they might leave Sage,

00:21:57.060 --> 00:21:59.160
they might go start their own business,

00:21:59.160 --> 00:22:00.746
10 years time, seven years time,

00:22:00.746 --> 00:22:03.146
they're always welcome
to come back to Sage,

00:22:03.146 --> 00:22:05.085
so it's not like the old days,

00:22:05.085 --> 00:22:07.835
you have a career in one company,

00:22:09.817 --> 00:22:11.691
I think it's great for the economies

00:22:11.691 --> 00:22:15.368
for individuals who are
fantastic entrepreneurs

00:22:15.368 --> 00:22:19.299
to really be able to express
their business freedom

00:22:19.299 --> 00:22:23.857
and really grow a lot of job
creation and wealth creation

00:22:23.857 --> 00:22:25.639
by goin' to start your business

00:22:25.639 --> 00:22:28.156
and then at the right
time you can come back,

00:22:28.156 --> 00:22:32.108
we need people who've got real experience

00:22:32.108 --> 00:22:34.502
of livin' the life of an entrepreneur,

00:22:34.502 --> 00:22:35.335
goin' for a start-up,

00:22:35.335 --> 00:22:37.527
worryin' about makin' payroll for a month,

00:22:37.527 --> 00:22:40.165
all that sort of stuff,
runnin' out of cash

00:22:40.165 --> 00:22:42.677
because the more we have
people who have lived

00:22:42.677 --> 00:22:45.417
and walked in the shoes of our customers

00:22:45.417 --> 00:22:49.704
then the more authentic we're
going to be as a company,

00:22:49.704 --> 00:22:51.573
I'd really encourage you,

00:22:51.573 --> 00:22:54.300
connect yourself to a company as Evan said

00:22:54.300 --> 00:22:57.171
with the values that you respect

00:22:57.171 --> 00:23:00.427
and actually I think it
could be a great marriage.

00:23:00.427 --> 00:23:01.700
- I love it

00:23:01.700 --> 00:23:03.596
Next up is Chris,

00:23:03.596 --> 00:23:06.111
knowing what you know
today what advice would

00:23:06.111 --> 00:23:08.816
you have given yourself when
you were first starting,

00:23:08.816 --> 00:23:11.649
different planning or other ideas.

00:23:12.995 --> 00:23:14.060
- That's a great question Chris.

00:23:14.060 --> 00:23:16.560
I think I would've number one,

00:23:18.640 --> 00:23:21.148
I would've absolutely my
idea would've focused on,

00:23:21.148 --> 00:23:24.545
what does the audience need,
what does the customer need,

00:23:24.545 --> 00:23:26.207
where do we find them?

00:23:26.207 --> 00:23:28.891
The other great advice I'd give
myself is surround yourself

00:23:28.891 --> 00:23:30.245
with great people

00:23:30.245 --> 00:23:31.786
and people who challenge you

00:23:31.786 --> 00:23:35.091
but people who are on the
same moon mission as you are,

00:23:35.091 --> 00:23:38.385
the people with the same
vision, the same ambition,

00:23:38.385 --> 00:23:41.399
the people who want to
literally fly to the moon

00:23:41.399 --> 00:23:43.615
and if you do that,

00:23:43.615 --> 00:23:45.286
you're probably going
to be hugely successful

00:23:45.286 --> 00:23:47.977
and it might not be necessarily
on your first venture

00:23:47.977 --> 00:23:52.144
but you'll come across as you
evolve your idea, huge success

00:23:53.888 --> 00:23:57.110
and in the context of this in confidence,

00:23:57.110 --> 00:24:01.775
be really happy if you
do fail fast learn quick

00:24:01.775 --> 00:24:04.192
and you'll do great business.

00:24:05.149 --> 00:24:07.296
- This is a hard one for
me because I'm so happy

00:24:07.296 --> 00:24:09.263
and grateful for where I am now

00:24:09.263 --> 00:24:12.844
that everything that
led up to this moment,

00:24:12.844 --> 00:24:13.923
has shaped who I am,

00:24:13.923 --> 00:24:16.146
If I change something in the
past would I still be here?

00:24:16.146 --> 00:24:18.755
That's the internal battle I have

00:24:18.755 --> 00:24:20.210
when I get asked this question

00:24:20.210 --> 00:24:22.066
but I would probably,
if I had to answer it

00:24:22.066 --> 00:24:25.066
would say believe sooner and harder,

00:24:26.316 --> 00:24:28.361
finding belief and going all in on it

00:24:28.361 --> 00:24:30.742
has had such a tremendous
impact on my life

00:24:30.742 --> 00:24:31.884
and on my business

00:24:31.884 --> 00:24:35.132
and it's been a newer
phenomenon in my life,

00:24:35.132 --> 00:24:39.178
maybe three, four, five
years something like that,

00:24:39.178 --> 00:24:41.898
If I discovered it sooner
and went in on it faster

00:24:41.898 --> 00:24:45.323
and was bolder and had
more confidence in it

00:24:45.323 --> 00:24:47.260
that would probably be,

00:24:47.260 --> 00:24:49.616
hopefully further ahead
then where I'm at now

00:24:49.616 --> 00:24:53.783
but at the same time I'm
really grateful for my spot.

00:24:55.041 --> 00:24:57.147
- Also Evan you probably did this,

00:24:57.147 --> 00:24:59.772
you're a risk taker in some ways,

00:24:59.772 --> 00:25:03.182
I think I'd say be a
responsible risk taker,

00:25:03.182 --> 00:25:05.659
I remember one job where
I was workin' really early

00:25:05.659 --> 00:25:08.162
on in my career for a big safe company

00:25:08.162 --> 00:25:11.579
and I resigned to go to a crazy start-up,

00:25:12.424 --> 00:25:14.794
that did become a really
successful company

00:25:14.794 --> 00:25:17.404
but I went back for the weekend
to talk to my mom and dad,

00:25:17.404 --> 00:25:20.335
they were alive at the
time and I said to Mom,

00:25:20.335 --> 00:25:23.811
Mom I'm going to go and
do this and she said

00:25:23.811 --> 00:25:25.663
well who's this company you're leavin'

00:25:25.663 --> 00:25:28.001
I know the company you're
with but why would you

00:25:28.001 --> 00:25:30.292
go work for a small start-up

00:25:30.292 --> 00:25:33.333
and she said you're absolutely crazy,

00:25:33.333 --> 00:25:36.505
you've got security, you've got a pension,

00:25:36.505 --> 00:25:38.088
I was 24 years old,

00:25:40.743 --> 00:25:44.589
with the benefit I saw, I
didn't take enough risks

00:25:44.589 --> 00:25:47.556
in my early career,
you'll get loads of people

00:25:47.556 --> 00:25:51.613
tellin' you the reasons
why you shouldn't do stuff

00:25:51.613 --> 00:25:53.990
but I think it comes back
to your inner confidence

00:25:53.990 --> 00:25:57.486
and conviction to say if
that's what you believe in,

00:25:57.486 --> 00:26:01.403
then shoot for those
stars and take more risks.

00:26:03.795 --> 00:26:07.333
- So it's an interesting
question because a lot of people

00:26:07.333 --> 00:26:10.250
get that, not necessarily the hate,

00:26:12.099 --> 00:26:13.100
not the people who are jealous,

00:26:13.100 --> 00:26:14.374
but it's the people who
are still close to you

00:26:14.374 --> 00:26:16.943
like your parents, your
friends telling you not

00:26:16.943 --> 00:26:18.607
to go and do something

00:26:18.607 --> 00:26:20.323
and for a lot of people
that holds them back

00:26:20.323 --> 00:26:22.148
'cause they really respect
and they love their mom

00:26:22.148 --> 00:26:23.722
and they want to make them proud,

00:26:23.722 --> 00:26:26.055
and carry family traditions,

00:26:27.451 --> 00:26:31.400
what's your advice to people
who want to pursue a path

00:26:31.400 --> 00:26:34.293
but their parents are blocking them?

00:26:34.293 --> 00:26:35.973
- That's a good question and
the other thing at the time,

00:26:35.973 --> 00:26:39.023
all my buddies thought I
was crazy for doin' it,

00:26:39.023 --> 00:26:40.551
'cause I said I'm goin' to a start-up,

00:26:40.551 --> 00:26:43.116
I'll probably work 60, 70, 80 hours a week

00:26:43.116 --> 00:26:45.078
and they said well we
won't see you anymore,

00:26:45.078 --> 00:26:46.743
you won't be able to come
down to the pub for drinks,

00:26:46.743 --> 00:26:49.674
I said well I'll still come in maybe,

00:26:49.674 --> 00:26:51.401
but I'll get here at 10
o'clock in the evenin'

00:26:51.401 --> 00:26:53.523
instead of six o'clock

00:26:53.523 --> 00:26:56.843
but we'll find a way to keep
those relationships in tact

00:26:56.843 --> 00:26:59.617
I think the key thing is,

00:26:59.617 --> 00:27:02.327
I'll directly answer
to your question Evan,

00:27:02.327 --> 00:27:05.472
The best advice I have at that age at 24,

00:27:05.472 --> 00:27:07.722
'cause I went and said look
everybody thinks I'm crazy

00:27:07.722 --> 00:27:10.221
goin' and joinin' a start-up,

00:27:10.221 --> 00:27:14.517
this guy I'd worked with and
knew as sort of a mentor,

00:27:14.517 --> 00:27:17.926
he said to me Stephen
what's the worst case

00:27:17.926 --> 00:27:19.291
that can happen?

00:27:19.291 --> 00:27:21.019
The worst case that can happen in a year

00:27:21.019 --> 00:27:23.952
is the start-up folds or they fire you

00:27:23.952 --> 00:27:25.647
and you know what you
can knock on the door

00:27:25.647 --> 00:27:28.166
of the company you've left
and they'd welcome you back

00:27:28.166 --> 00:27:30.942
with open arms and actually
you would've gained even

00:27:30.942 --> 00:27:33.342
more experience and great life experience

00:27:33.342 --> 00:27:36.750
from dealing with that failure
and learning from that,

00:27:36.750 --> 00:27:41.204
you would've been a much
better entrepreneur yourself,

00:27:41.204 --> 00:27:43.845
when you look at it rationally

00:27:43.845 --> 00:27:46.643
and you sort of divorce
yourself from the moment,

00:27:46.643 --> 00:27:49.283
and you say well what
could really go wrong here?

00:27:49.283 --> 00:27:51.282
If it didn't work out,
you can always go back

00:27:51.282 --> 00:27:52.575
to where you were.

00:27:52.575 --> 00:27:54.080
I think the way you have
to deal with this Evan,

00:27:54.080 --> 00:27:56.062
is you have to be true to yourself

00:27:56.062 --> 00:27:58.805
and it kind of comes back to the essence

00:27:58.805 --> 00:28:00.940
of where you get your confidence.

00:28:00.940 --> 00:28:04.440
No one here is livin' someone else's life.

00:28:05.799 --> 00:28:08.638
It's not that profound
but we only get one shot,

00:28:08.638 --> 00:28:11.475
it's not a rehearsal and you've
got to make the most of it

00:28:11.475 --> 00:28:14.975
and the only thing that flies past is time

00:28:18.134 --> 00:28:19.689
and when you got the
energy and you're young

00:28:19.689 --> 00:28:21.316
and you can work flat out

00:28:21.316 --> 00:28:23.339
and you might not have responsibilities,

00:28:23.339 --> 00:28:27.038
it's a fantastic time to
really pursue your passion

00:28:27.038 --> 00:28:29.538
and really follow your dreams,

00:28:31.161 --> 00:28:34.630
the folks who are listenin' today,

00:28:34.630 --> 00:28:38.202
it would be a time if you go
home to your mom, your dad,

00:28:38.202 --> 00:28:42.903
even friends and they say,
the nay sayers, don't do it,

00:28:42.903 --> 00:28:46.010
if you really believe
in it then absolutely

00:28:46.010 --> 00:28:48.969
have the conviction to go and do it.

00:28:48.969 --> 00:28:50.807
You've got to live your own life.

00:28:50.807 --> 00:28:53.405
- I like it, I like it.

00:28:53.405 --> 00:28:55.068
Here's an interesting one,

00:28:55.068 --> 00:28:57.399
gettin' on the conversation
with this guest Anna,

00:28:57.399 --> 00:28:58.966
she's in our chat.

00:28:58.966 --> 00:29:01.270
She was just praising us and saying Evan,

00:29:01.270 --> 00:29:03.652
you're a natural host slash interviewer

00:29:03.652 --> 00:29:06.717
and I wrote back to say
it's not natural at all,

00:29:06.717 --> 00:29:08.998
it's all learned behavior,

00:29:08.998 --> 00:29:11.908
when I first started
doing this I was terrible

00:29:11.908 --> 00:29:13.250
and I'm curious for you,

00:29:13.250 --> 00:29:16.431
some of the things that
people may look at you

00:29:16.431 --> 00:29:20.137
and say man Stephen is so talented at this

00:29:20.137 --> 00:29:22.810
but really when you
started you had no idea

00:29:22.810 --> 00:29:24.311
and you were failin', you were messin' up

00:29:24.311 --> 00:29:27.118
and it's just a result
of decades of hard work,

00:29:27.118 --> 00:29:29.215
is there something that comes to mind

00:29:29.215 --> 00:29:32.282
that you're praised for and
people might think is natural

00:29:32.282 --> 00:29:35.889
but is really just a ton of
hard work that went into it?

00:29:35.889 --> 00:29:37.959
- Yeah I think,

00:29:37.959 --> 00:29:42.345
this is true and I think even
as you get older in life,

00:29:42.345 --> 00:29:45.888
your soul remains the same,
your passion remains the same,

00:29:45.888 --> 00:29:50.057
your energy and your moral
compass is always authentic

00:29:50.057 --> 00:29:52.019
and remains constant.

00:29:52.019 --> 00:29:55.647
The thing that, directly
to your point Evan,

00:29:55.647 --> 00:30:00.098
for me personally a lot of
things I've learned through

00:30:00.098 --> 00:30:02.546
a bitter experience to gettin' on TV

00:30:02.546 --> 00:30:04.826
and screwin' up on an interview.

00:30:04.826 --> 00:30:07.193
But boy oh boy did I learn,

00:30:07.193 --> 00:30:09.060
when I've made mistakes,

00:30:09.060 --> 00:30:13.167
they've been the best
learnin's I've taken away.

00:30:13.167 --> 00:30:15.880
The other thing relative to confidence,

00:30:15.880 --> 00:30:18.630
is the more you practice
the better you get,

00:30:18.630 --> 00:30:21.533
and you'll notice Michael
Jordan all these statements

00:30:21.533 --> 00:30:24.929
about the number of times
they've missed a winnin' shot

00:30:24.929 --> 00:30:26.508
in a game or whatever.

00:30:26.508 --> 00:30:28.811
The more they practice,
the better they get.

00:30:28.811 --> 00:30:31.502
I think it was Rory Mciiroy
and then Gary Player,

00:30:31.502 --> 00:30:35.461
golfers said the more I
practice, the luckier I am.

00:30:35.461 --> 00:30:36.854
And it's the same in business

00:30:36.854 --> 00:30:39.269
and I think Anna to your point,

00:30:39.269 --> 00:30:41.263
look at me what was I?

00:30:41.263 --> 00:30:43.271
When I was probably your age Anna,

00:30:43.271 --> 00:30:47.438
I was a spotty not very
attractive, not very confident,

00:30:49.467 --> 00:30:54.411
but I just was kind of, I wanted
to be not a street fighter,

00:30:54.411 --> 00:30:55.727
but in the world of entrepreneurship,

00:30:55.727 --> 00:30:57.734
I wanted to get out there, I had a dream,

00:30:57.734 --> 00:31:01.349
I had a passion and I
was happy to hunker down

00:31:01.349 --> 00:31:05.082
and really work hard
because I really believed in

00:31:05.082 --> 00:31:06.438
what I was tryin' to achieve,

00:31:06.438 --> 00:31:08.659
for ultimately the customers I served

00:31:08.659 --> 00:31:10.812
and to create a market place.

00:31:10.812 --> 00:31:13.366
When I look back, yeah I've learned

00:31:13.366 --> 00:31:17.001
and obviously Evan I think is a brilliant,

00:31:17.001 --> 00:31:19.534
he should be a chat
show up with Trevor Noah

00:31:19.534 --> 00:31:21.572
and these sort of guys,
I think he's up there

00:31:21.572 --> 00:31:23.588
with the best of them.

00:31:23.588 --> 00:31:25.588
But a lot of it is a
bit of learned behavior,

00:31:25.588 --> 00:31:28.444
but I think what is constant,

00:31:28.444 --> 00:31:30.777
is the essence of who you are

00:31:30.777 --> 00:31:33.771
and your soul is always the same

00:31:33.771 --> 00:31:36.312
and that's why ultimately
people buy from you,

00:31:36.312 --> 00:31:39.113
that's why people want to work for you,

00:31:39.113 --> 00:31:41.035
that's why people want to work with you.

00:31:41.035 --> 00:31:43.019
That's why partners
want to build something

00:31:43.019 --> 00:31:46.039
special, unique and magical with you,

00:31:46.039 --> 00:31:49.003
you want to hang on to
that and the confidence

00:31:49.003 --> 00:31:51.170
really amplifies your soul

00:31:52.375 --> 00:31:55.939
and your physical presence and
whether you do hand gestures

00:31:55.939 --> 00:31:59.194
or whether good openings
on presentational,

00:31:59.194 --> 00:32:01.210
whether you got skills in terms

00:32:01.210 --> 00:32:04.464
of blamps and google hangouts and stuff.

00:32:04.464 --> 00:32:06.665
The only thing I'd say
there is the more you do,

00:32:06.665 --> 00:32:09.254
the better you get, the more you practice

00:32:09.254 --> 00:32:12.436
and the one vital piece of
magic that I learned in life

00:32:12.436 --> 00:32:14.531
is two things really,

00:32:14.531 --> 00:32:17.198
one is empathy, put
yourself in shoes of people

00:32:17.198 --> 00:32:18.187
you're tryin' to serve

00:32:18.187 --> 00:32:21.659
and the second thing is
just always with two ears,

00:32:21.659 --> 00:32:23.659
one mouth take feedback.

00:32:24.695 --> 00:32:27.067
If people are prepared to invest in you

00:32:27.067 --> 00:32:29.925
by giving you feedback even
though it can hurt you,

00:32:29.925 --> 00:32:31.983
and it can cut to the core,

00:32:31.983 --> 00:32:34.375
take that feedback,
learn from that feedback,

00:32:34.375 --> 00:32:35.958
'cause it is magic.

00:32:37.731 --> 00:32:39.196
- I love it, I love it.

00:32:39.196 --> 00:32:40.363
Spittin' fire.

