00:35:27.009 --> 00:35:29.528
Cynna, sometimes I get tired

00:35:29.528 --> 00:35:32.640
of pursuing my passion of rapping,

00:35:32.640 --> 00:35:34.727
I'm wondering right now
should I take a break

00:35:34.727 --> 00:35:37.199
or keep at it, if so how long,

00:35:37.199 --> 00:35:41.366
I get afraid of fallin'
behind, much love to you guys.

00:35:43.156 --> 00:35:44.716
- That's a good question Cynna,

00:35:44.716 --> 00:35:48.067
I think if you're lovin' what you're doin'

00:35:48.067 --> 00:35:50.067
whatever it is, rappin',

00:35:51.041 --> 00:35:54.790
you probably need to say
are you really energized,

00:35:54.790 --> 00:35:56.165
are you fresh?

00:35:56.165 --> 00:35:59.972
Are you really focused
on achieving your goals

00:35:59.972 --> 00:36:02.479
and invariably there comes a time

00:36:02.479 --> 00:36:05.012
where you run out of energy

00:36:05.012 --> 00:36:08.890
and you need to have a bit
of recharge and reflection

00:36:08.890 --> 00:36:11.430
so at those point in times,

00:36:11.430 --> 00:36:15.566
whatever works for you, you
could have a vacation whatever,

00:36:15.566 --> 00:36:18.060
you get together with friends,
you go and see mentors,

00:36:18.060 --> 00:36:21.039
whatever it is that really works for you

00:36:21.039 --> 00:36:24.192
and then I think that's a great time

00:36:24.192 --> 00:36:26.899
to reflect on what's workin'

00:36:26.899 --> 00:36:30.516
fundamentally just still
believe in your big idea,

00:36:30.516 --> 00:36:32.096
If you do what do you need to change

00:36:32.096 --> 00:36:33.795
to make it even more successful

00:36:33.795 --> 00:36:37.730
and then you can kind of
get right back into it.

00:36:37.730 --> 00:36:38.687
- This is an interesting one

00:36:38.687 --> 00:36:40.684
'cause we're just about to release a video

00:36:40.684 --> 00:36:43.010
on Lindsay Sterling, I don't
know if you know Lindsay,

00:36:43.010 --> 00:36:45.760
she's a violinist and she had one

00:36:47.128 --> 00:36:51.640
of the top YouTube videos
for one of her songs,

00:36:51.640 --> 00:36:53.504
and when she was doin' violin,

00:36:53.504 --> 00:36:56.155
she kind of got bored of it

00:36:56.155 --> 00:36:58.346
'cause it was kind of classical

00:36:58.346 --> 00:37:01.222
and the same thing and she
just grew a little tired of it

00:37:01.222 --> 00:37:02.746
and she wanted to put it down

00:37:02.746 --> 00:37:05.986
and then she discovered
electronic music and Skrillex

00:37:05.986 --> 00:37:08.977
and Djs and all these
people putting out beats

00:37:08.977 --> 00:37:13.416
and she said that'd be really
cool to combine my violin

00:37:13.416 --> 00:37:16.582
with the electronic beats
and she formed a new thing

00:37:16.582 --> 00:37:17.805
that hadn't existed before

00:37:17.805 --> 00:37:21.150
and that reignited her
interest and passion in violin

00:37:21.150 --> 00:37:22.832
and also helped her differentiate herself

00:37:22.832 --> 00:37:26.492
and create one of the top
videos for music videos,

00:37:26.492 --> 00:37:28.545
so I'm wondering if there
might be something like

00:37:28.545 --> 00:37:29.976
that in it for you,

00:37:29.976 --> 00:37:31.629
where if you still love rapping

00:37:31.629 --> 00:37:34.293
but you don't like doin' it
the way you've been doin' it

00:37:34.293 --> 00:37:36.072
or the way other people are doin' it,

00:37:36.072 --> 00:37:39.289
to explore maybe combine it
with another passion of yours

00:37:39.289 --> 00:37:42.517
to continue to test the waters.

00:37:42.517 --> 00:37:44.456
If you look at The Beatles,

00:37:44.456 --> 00:37:47.217
none of their albums
sound like each other,

00:37:47.217 --> 00:37:49.307
the first album was way
different than the next one

00:37:49.307 --> 00:37:50.550
and way different than the next one

00:37:50.550 --> 00:37:53.067
and people are like we
like the early Beatles,

00:37:53.067 --> 00:37:54.202
why don't you keep doing those,

00:37:54.202 --> 00:37:57.141
well we've grown as people,
we don't do that anymore,

00:37:57.141 --> 00:37:58.584
we want to do new stuff

00:37:58.584 --> 00:38:00.559
and I think that's part
of every entrepreneur,

00:38:00.559 --> 00:38:03.030
especially artists, you
have to continue to evolve

00:38:03.030 --> 00:38:05.169
and test and try new things

00:38:05.169 --> 00:38:06.886
and so if you're getting a little bored

00:38:06.886 --> 00:38:08.011
of what you're doing,

00:38:08.011 --> 00:38:11.178
I'd say how do I mix in something new?

00:38:12.654 --> 00:38:14.769
Try out a new project that
still involves the rapping,

00:38:14.769 --> 00:38:16.529
but you can involve and
incorporate something else

00:38:16.529 --> 00:38:20.696
in it to push yourself and
see if it makes it come alive.

00:38:21.601 --> 00:38:25.027
- I think also if you're
really gettin' bored,

00:38:25.027 --> 00:38:29.194
go round and listen to
rappers you really respect,

00:38:30.092 --> 00:38:32.557
go and meet some of them,

00:38:32.557 --> 00:38:35.494
maybe think about doing
something collaboratively

00:38:35.494 --> 00:38:38.993
with a partner 'cause it sounds like

00:38:38.993 --> 00:38:40.287
you need new innovation,

00:38:40.287 --> 00:38:42.698
sounds like you got a core
idea, you love what you're doin'

00:38:42.698 --> 00:38:45.731
but if you need to evolve it
there's loads of opportunities

00:38:45.731 --> 00:38:48.648
and Evan, Atlanta, May 9th to 11th.

00:38:50.655 --> 00:38:52.590
Sage Summit Atlanta.

00:38:52.590 --> 00:38:56.153
- Well we'll both be there
so Adarsh come find us in May

00:38:56.153 --> 00:38:59.849
in Atlanta, that's just
before my birthday I'm May 20

00:38:59.849 --> 00:39:03.841
so we'll celebrate in Atlanta together.

00:39:03.841 --> 00:39:06.820
Sherry is writing in
talkin' about her story,

00:39:06.820 --> 00:39:10.367
saying her story is too long
every time she tries to tell it

00:39:10.367 --> 00:39:12.047
and she's havin' a hard time cutting

00:39:12.047 --> 00:39:13.730
it down to the essentials,

00:39:13.730 --> 00:39:15.912
so when you're tellin'
your story to people,

00:39:15.912 --> 00:39:20.079
how do you make it impactful
without talkin' for too long.

00:39:21.407 --> 00:39:24.411
Probably the first thing
is think about the audience

00:39:24.411 --> 00:39:28.578
you're tryin' to serve, so I
mentioned that word empathy,

00:39:28.578 --> 00:39:32.217
what problem are you tryin'
to solve, what pain point?

00:39:32.217 --> 00:39:35.231
If you can tell 'em your
story reflectin' against

00:39:35.231 --> 00:39:39.314
their needs and really
speakin' for their hearts.

00:39:40.324 --> 00:39:42.418
Then I think you're on a winner.

00:39:42.418 --> 00:39:45.320
Then make it really simple,
use language that's simple,

00:39:45.320 --> 00:39:49.237
understandable, compellin',
exciting, evocative

00:39:50.075 --> 00:39:52.011
and you are absolutely unaware,

00:39:52.011 --> 00:39:54.303
I just think when I look at the companies

00:39:54.303 --> 00:39:56.033
that have been turned down for money,

00:39:56.033 --> 00:39:57.955
some of them because they probably had

00:39:57.955 --> 00:39:59.031
to refine their stories,

00:39:59.031 --> 00:40:00.124
there's tons of 'em,

00:40:00.124 --> 00:40:03.791
like Tangle Teaser
revenue's now 23 million,

00:40:05.902 --> 00:40:06.790
they got turned down,

00:40:06.790 --> 00:40:09.784
another Trunkie revenues
10 million dollars,

00:40:09.784 --> 00:40:13.123
all these companies have been out there,

00:40:13.123 --> 00:40:14.400
either a stage where they've gone

00:40:14.400 --> 00:40:16.876
through friends and
family and your investin',

00:40:16.876 --> 00:40:21.199
tryin' to raise money and
the key thing was their pitch

00:40:21.199 --> 00:40:24.112
had to be evolved and
shortened and crispened

00:40:24.112 --> 00:40:25.768
so it is really good,

00:40:25.768 --> 00:40:28.784
the other thing around this
I think was fair to say,

00:40:28.784 --> 00:40:31.119
just give 'em enough of the story

00:40:31.119 --> 00:40:33.760
to tease 'em for the next chapter.

00:40:33.760 --> 00:40:36.483
Don't try and read the whole book at once,

00:40:36.483 --> 00:40:38.388
just tease 'em and what you really want

00:40:38.388 --> 00:40:41.246
with a great story, you
want the forward which

00:40:41.246 --> 00:40:44.142
is whatever elevator pitch,
whatever you want to call it.

00:40:44.142 --> 00:40:47.102
It's compellin', evocative,
audience centered,

00:40:47.102 --> 00:40:49.705
and it just gives enough to say wow,

00:40:49.705 --> 00:40:51.329
I want to go to the next stage

00:40:51.329 --> 00:40:53.018
and then the next stage is the next stage

00:40:53.018 --> 00:40:56.778
and then they open the rich
fabric of the whole book,

00:40:56.778 --> 00:40:58.361
chapter by chapter.

00:40:59.246 --> 00:41:01.482
- Just like bein' a good sales person,

00:41:01.482 --> 00:41:02.942
the best sales people use their ears

00:41:02.942 --> 00:41:04.736
more than their mouth.

00:41:04.736 --> 00:41:05.839
You want to be a good story teller,

00:41:05.839 --> 00:41:07.993
it starts by listening before talking,

00:41:07.993 --> 00:41:10.387
understanding who they
are and what they need,

00:41:10.387 --> 00:41:12.451
'cause you have a thousand
stories you can tell,

00:41:12.451 --> 00:41:14.067
Stephen and I could
tell a thousand stories

00:41:14.067 --> 00:41:16.018
about different things from out past

00:41:16.018 --> 00:41:18.511
but it's picking the three
out of those thousand

00:41:18.511 --> 00:41:21.907
that's going to make an impact
on this person in front of you

00:41:21.907 --> 00:41:23.905
and so if you have no idea
who you're talkin' to,

00:41:23.905 --> 00:41:26.981
it makes it harder, you could
pick your most effective story

00:41:26.981 --> 00:41:29.802
but if you have a little bit
more context in who they are,

00:41:29.802 --> 00:41:30.766
what they're going through,

00:41:30.766 --> 00:41:33.374
then you can select the
right stories from your past

00:41:33.374 --> 00:41:35.510
that will have a meaningful impact on them

00:41:35.510 --> 00:41:39.170
so you don't go to long
and get really boring.

00:41:39.170 --> 00:41:41.087
- Just a final thing, Evan on that is,

00:41:41.087 --> 00:41:44.914
I always love to listen to
stories that tell me about

00:41:44.914 --> 00:41:46.992
how customers have been successful,

00:41:46.992 --> 00:41:50.465
so if you're at a stage where
you got some early customers,

00:41:50.465 --> 00:41:54.196
go and listen to Evan's
point, to those customers

00:41:54.196 --> 00:41:57.155
and they will tell your
story better than anybody

00:41:57.155 --> 00:41:59.960
could in the world and then
you want to bottle that

00:41:59.960 --> 00:42:02.570
and if you can tell a customer a story,

00:42:02.570 --> 00:42:04.262
of your product, of your service,

00:42:04.262 --> 00:42:05.612
how it's changed their lives,

00:42:05.612 --> 00:42:07.403
how it's made their lives easier,

00:42:07.403 --> 00:42:10.588
you're on a winner, you're
absolutely unstoppable,

00:42:10.588 --> 00:42:13.482
get customers to tell your story for you.

00:42:13.482 --> 00:42:14.849
- I love it.

00:42:14.849 --> 00:42:18.295
- Next up we got a couple
people asking the same question.

00:42:18.295 --> 00:42:20.767
Tinmag is sayin' I have
a lot of ideas in my life

00:42:20.767 --> 00:42:23.697
and for my business, how do
I decide which is the best

00:42:23.697 --> 00:42:25.391
one to go for?

00:42:25.391 --> 00:42:27.727
Brian asking kind of the same thing,

00:42:27.727 --> 00:42:29.933
my question is what if you
have more than one dream,

00:42:29.933 --> 00:42:31.180
how do you pick the best one?

00:42:31.180 --> 00:42:34.990
So Tinmag and Brian basically
got lots of potential ideas

00:42:34.990 --> 00:42:38.630
how do you find the one that's
going to be best for you?

00:42:38.630 --> 00:42:40.331
- Good question Tinmag, Brian

00:42:40.331 --> 00:42:44.611
and I think you're obviously
people with great ideas,

00:42:44.611 --> 00:42:46.611
sounds like lots of 'em.

00:42:50.518 --> 00:42:52.367
If you want to be an entrepreneur,

00:42:52.367 --> 00:42:54.958
what is it that really motivates you,

00:42:54.958 --> 00:42:57.606
do you want to be a billionaire,

00:42:57.606 --> 00:43:00.287
do you want to be the next Zuckerberg

00:43:00.287 --> 00:43:01.266
or something like that

00:43:01.266 --> 00:43:03.194
or do you want to do
it to change the world

00:43:03.194 --> 00:43:05.746
or do you want to do it
cause you're alteristic,

00:43:05.746 --> 00:43:08.206
do you want to do it 'cause you
want to change your community,

00:43:08.206 --> 00:43:09.807
you got to decide what the motivate

00:43:09.807 --> 00:43:14.430
and then against that rank
in terms of your mission,

00:43:14.430 --> 00:43:16.888
then you can start sayin' well if

00:43:16.888 --> 00:43:19.069
that's what the end game is,

00:43:19.069 --> 00:43:22.315
what's the best idea to
actually get to that end game?

00:43:22.315 --> 00:43:24.636
Again sort of stayin' true to yourself

00:43:24.636 --> 00:43:26.949
and havin' the confidence to pursue it

00:43:26.949 --> 00:43:28.543
and then there's the obvious stuff,

00:43:28.543 --> 00:43:31.987
if you're drivin' it to build
a business and monetize it,

00:43:31.987 --> 00:43:33.977
it's lookin' at what's
the market opportunity,

00:43:33.977 --> 00:43:37.053
how do you change the market,
how do you be disruptive,

00:43:37.053 --> 00:43:39.136
from that you can put
the bones in the skeleton

00:43:39.136 --> 00:43:40.843
of a business plan together

00:43:40.843 --> 00:43:43.349
and then you just ask yourself
what do you need to do

00:43:43.349 --> 00:43:45.170
to execute against that vision,

00:43:45.170 --> 00:43:47.670
now the question than becomes,

00:43:49.794 --> 00:43:52.328
if you wrote Evan and my epitaph

00:43:52.328 --> 00:43:55.699
it would be one life was never enough.

00:43:55.699 --> 00:43:57.929
One life was never enough.

00:43:57.929 --> 00:44:00.478
You have to be ruthless with your time,

00:44:00.478 --> 00:44:01.901
'cause the most valuable thing

00:44:01.901 --> 00:44:05.478
is your energy, your
passion and your time.

00:44:05.478 --> 00:44:07.032
So then you can ask yourself,

00:44:07.032 --> 00:44:11.625
'cause you might be able
to start two or three ideas

00:44:11.625 --> 00:44:12.907
and start them simultaneously

00:44:12.907 --> 00:44:14.992
but you need to be careful there

00:44:14.992 --> 00:44:17.940
that you're not tryin' to
spread yourself too thinly

00:44:17.940 --> 00:44:19.823
and you might find if you
spread yourself too thinly,

00:44:19.823 --> 00:44:21.573
all three ideas fail.

00:44:23.232 --> 00:44:25.112
What you might find if
you only focus on one,

00:44:25.112 --> 00:44:28.773
pick the winner, if it's
monetization it's easy to evaluate,

00:44:28.773 --> 00:44:31.153
if it's other criteria you're drivin',

00:44:31.153 --> 00:44:34.641
just pick the one that's
authentic and closest

00:44:34.641 --> 00:44:37.717
to what you're tryin' to
drive in terms of your values,

00:44:37.717 --> 00:44:39.477
but if you can happy days,

00:44:39.477 --> 00:44:43.361
say you can drive three
business ideas concurrently

00:44:43.361 --> 00:44:45.329
then it's all about just pickin' a winner,

00:44:45.329 --> 00:44:48.873
and you'll find through the
momentum in the business ideas,

00:44:48.873 --> 00:44:51.625
the one that really gains
traction will be the one

00:44:51.625 --> 00:44:55.102
that you really want to double
down on and really focus on,

00:44:55.102 --> 00:44:57.891
I think there's no perfect answer to this

00:44:57.891 --> 00:45:00.501
but the question you got
to be true to yourself

00:45:00.501 --> 00:45:02.473
and it's that moment on a Friday night

00:45:02.473 --> 00:45:05.863
when you get home maybe have
a glass of wine or a beer,

00:45:05.863 --> 00:45:08.812
pick the winners always, pick the winners

00:45:08.812 --> 00:45:11.276
and then be ruthless can I just do one,

00:45:11.276 --> 00:45:13.520
can I do two, can I do three,

00:45:13.520 --> 00:45:14.693
can you get some of your buddies

00:45:14.693 --> 00:45:16.668
to start some of your ideas for you,

00:45:16.668 --> 00:45:18.786
be involved as an investor,

00:45:18.786 --> 00:45:19.986
get them goin'

00:45:19.986 --> 00:45:22.693
and then ultimately what you want to do

00:45:22.693 --> 00:45:25.973
is fundamentally have the
confidence to pick a winner,

00:45:25.973 --> 00:45:28.835
double down on it and
really grow a great business

00:45:28.835 --> 00:45:30.918
and express your passion.

00:45:33.418 --> 00:45:36.599
- I would first similar
with what Stephen's saying,

00:45:36.599 --> 00:45:39.296
examine the intentions and
make sure that it's pure,

00:45:39.296 --> 00:45:44.124
you're doing this because this
has to exist in the world,

00:45:44.124 --> 00:45:45.854
you've got the passion for it,

00:45:45.854 --> 00:45:47.668
it's not on your list of
ideas because you opened

00:45:47.668 --> 00:45:50.459
up a magazine and here's a
list of hottest companies

00:45:50.459 --> 00:45:52.800
and that's why you want
to go start and do it,

00:45:52.800 --> 00:45:54.322
it's got to come of a
place where you actually

00:45:54.322 --> 00:45:56.232
have the passion for it.

00:45:56.232 --> 00:45:58.163
If your reasons are pure

00:45:58.163 --> 00:45:59.569
and you've still got multiple ideas,

00:45:59.569 --> 00:46:00.962
I would actually look for,

00:46:00.962 --> 00:46:04.651
how can I combine these things
together into one business

00:46:04.651 --> 00:46:06.489
and use that as my differentiator,

00:46:06.489 --> 00:46:07.627
so lookin' at Lindsay Sterling

00:46:07.627 --> 00:46:10.475
that we talked about, she likes violining

00:46:10.475 --> 00:46:12.559
and she likes electronic music

00:46:12.559 --> 00:46:15.801
and those have not typically gone together

00:46:15.801 --> 00:46:18.376
and so by mashing them together

00:46:18.376 --> 00:46:19.907
now she's different and she's unique

00:46:19.907 --> 00:46:20.740
and she stands out

00:46:20.740 --> 00:46:21.691
and she's the only one doin' it,

00:46:21.691 --> 00:46:23.287
she found her own market,

00:46:23.287 --> 00:46:24.995
so I've been lookin' at
how can I combine some

00:46:24.995 --> 00:46:27.794
of these things together to
form the ultimate business

00:46:27.794 --> 00:46:29.073
that only you can do because

00:46:29.073 --> 00:46:31.990
you have these different interests.

00:46:33.504 --> 00:46:34.337
Good luck.

00:46:34.337 --> 00:46:35.801
But again make sure that
it has to be both things

00:46:35.801 --> 00:46:38.194
are what you are deeply passionate about,

00:46:38.194 --> 00:46:42.702
are true and pure and not
just chasing opportunity.

00:46:42.702 --> 00:46:43.713
- Great idea.

00:46:43.713 --> 00:46:44.546
Good idea.

00:46:44.546 --> 00:46:48.414
And if you can combine the
ideas that's even better.

00:46:48.414 --> 00:46:52.581
- William is writing in, he's
okay with talkin' to people,

00:46:54.575 --> 00:46:55.748
he's talkin' about story telling,

00:46:55.748 --> 00:46:58.780
he like talking with people
but if the conversation goes on

00:46:58.780 --> 00:47:01.644
for too long his mood goes down.

00:47:01.644 --> 00:47:03.766
So for people who are maybe introverted

00:47:03.766 --> 00:47:08.462
and don't like having long
conversations and it drains them,

00:47:08.462 --> 00:47:11.271
what advice do we have on story telling

00:47:11.271 --> 00:47:13.803
and being confident in conversations

00:47:13.803 --> 00:47:16.435
when you're more introverted.

00:47:16.435 --> 00:47:20.268
- I think all of us have
some degree of nature

00:47:21.985 --> 00:47:23.985
where we're introverted.

00:47:25.294 --> 00:47:28.544
The other thing, there's a unique skill

00:47:29.525 --> 00:47:32.551
to have a rich conversation.

00:47:32.551 --> 00:47:36.438
I think we get too
excited as entrepreneurs,

00:47:36.438 --> 00:47:39.212
we feel we need to pitch

00:47:39.212 --> 00:47:43.124
and as Evan said the real
uniqueness of great entrepreneurs

00:47:43.124 --> 00:47:46.023
in my experience is people
who listen really well,

00:47:46.023 --> 00:47:48.773
listen acutely, so maybe William,

00:47:49.621 --> 00:47:52.412
you just give 'em the value proposition,

00:47:52.412 --> 00:47:55.662
the compellin' reason why your big idea

00:47:56.509 --> 00:48:00.176
can add massive value
and change their world

00:48:01.038 --> 00:48:04.128
but then you just sit back
and you rely on some really,

00:48:04.128 --> 00:48:06.250
really wise friends that you have.

00:48:06.250 --> 00:48:09.374
Those friends ask just questions.

00:48:09.374 --> 00:48:12.569
You've heard my idea what do you think?

00:48:12.569 --> 00:48:15.043
How would this work for you?

00:48:15.043 --> 00:48:17.417
Then you just listen and you take feedback

00:48:17.417 --> 00:48:19.946
and then the interesting
thing you say William,

00:48:19.946 --> 00:48:22.895
your motivation goes
down if you keep pitchin'

00:48:22.895 --> 00:48:26.631
I think you get energized when
you listen to other people

00:48:26.631 --> 00:48:29.858
and you get their input
and you get real engagement

00:48:29.858 --> 00:48:34.118
and a rich interaction
and that's how you build

00:48:34.118 --> 00:48:38.285
your products and service to
be better and well beaten.

00:48:39.456 --> 00:48:43.594
The power of listenin'
is such a great skill

00:48:43.594 --> 00:48:45.846
an so often undervalued

00:48:45.846 --> 00:48:48.349
and it just for me personally,

00:48:48.349 --> 00:48:50.035
when I listen to whether
I go to customers,

00:48:50.035 --> 00:48:53.778
colleagues, partners, the
accountants we deal with,

00:48:53.778 --> 00:48:56.038
I find it really upliftin'

00:48:56.038 --> 00:48:59.528
and it gives me a real
sense of not only good karma

00:48:59.528 --> 00:49:01.361
but tremendous energy,

00:49:02.402 --> 00:49:05.089
if you find you're out there pitchin'

00:49:05.089 --> 00:49:08.547
turn the tables and start
askin' some wise questions

00:49:08.547 --> 00:49:10.489
about what does it look like for you?

00:49:10.489 --> 00:49:12.980
How do you feel about
what I've talked about?

00:49:12.980 --> 00:49:16.104
What's the context, how would
it solve a pain point for you

00:49:16.104 --> 00:49:20.527
and then just listen, then
the conversation flows

00:49:20.527 --> 00:49:22.944
and it'll be very energizing.

00:49:23.947 --> 00:49:28.717
- So William, I'm an introvert
that may not come across,

00:49:28.717 --> 00:49:31.824
you may not feel that from me but I am,

00:49:31.824 --> 00:49:33.747
a couple things that have helped me,

00:49:33.747 --> 00:49:38.429
one I'd limit my exposure
so I'd really chunk my time

00:49:38.429 --> 00:49:41.956
so Thursdays are my day
to be public facing,

00:49:41.956 --> 00:49:46.852
it's hangouts, I'm going into
four meetings after this,

00:49:46.852 --> 00:49:48.669
that's my Thursdays

00:49:48.669 --> 00:49:51.332
and then tomorrow is nothing,

00:49:51.332 --> 00:49:55.325
introvert day just work and
the rest of my week is similar

00:49:55.325 --> 00:49:56.594
where I'm not meeting people,

00:49:56.594 --> 00:49:58.503
I'm not having a lot of conversations

00:49:58.503 --> 00:50:01.352
and that works for me
so the one day a week

00:50:01.352 --> 00:50:05.216
where I'm my extroverted
self and then I can recover

00:50:05.216 --> 00:50:08.145
for the rest of the week,
may be helpful for you

00:50:08.145 --> 00:50:11.071
and then also design a
business model around

00:50:11.071 --> 00:50:12.995
how you can win,

00:50:12.995 --> 00:50:14.470
this theme is about having the confidence

00:50:14.470 --> 00:50:16.408
to tell your truths

00:50:16.408 --> 00:50:18.279
be yourself and tell your story.

00:50:18.279 --> 00:50:21.664
It doesn't mean you need to be
out networking 24 hours a day

00:50:21.664 --> 00:50:23.899
let the extroverts go out and do that.

00:50:23.899 --> 00:50:26.418
I win by creating content,

00:50:26.418 --> 00:50:29.336
I never go to events and conferences

00:50:29.336 --> 00:50:32.230
'cause I hate networking
and introducing myself,

00:50:32.230 --> 00:50:34.216
I'll only go if I'm
speaking 'cause then people

00:50:34.216 --> 00:50:36.687
will come up to me and I limit those too.

00:50:36.687 --> 00:50:39.364
So recognizing what you're great at,

00:50:39.364 --> 00:50:42.266
you can still tell your
story and create content,

00:50:42.266 --> 00:50:44.650
you can make a video or
you can write articles

00:50:44.650 --> 00:50:46.899
and you can push out on social media

00:50:46.899 --> 00:50:50.745
without having to go and
talk to people all the time.

00:50:50.745 --> 00:50:53.567
If my business relied on
me being a sales person,

00:50:53.567 --> 00:50:56.462
we would fail but luckily this is enough

00:50:56.462 --> 00:50:59.574
of marketing and content creation

00:50:59.574 --> 00:51:02.643
and all the other things
that could help me win

00:51:02.643 --> 00:51:04.633
that that's what I stand on.

00:51:04.633 --> 00:51:06.052
There's a lot of different
ways to build a business,

00:51:06.052 --> 00:51:09.052
you don't have to be the Mark Cuban,

00:51:10.272 --> 00:51:14.685
yelling in your face
super loud person to win,

00:51:14.685 --> 00:51:18.169
you can find your way to stand as well.

00:51:18.169 --> 00:51:21.756
- Evan what you say there is true.

00:51:21.756 --> 00:51:23.639
I probably won't articulate this very well

00:51:23.639 --> 00:51:27.151
but you capture something in the essence

00:51:27.151 --> 00:51:30.429
of the honesty and
authenticity of what you say,

00:51:30.429 --> 00:51:33.762
where you say look I'm not an introvert,

00:51:34.748 --> 00:51:36.703
in this case to William as well,

00:51:36.703 --> 00:51:38.999
sometimes if you're doin' a pitch,

00:51:38.999 --> 00:51:40.923
I would feel pretty comfortable

00:51:40.923 --> 00:51:42.799
with someone doin' a
pitch to me sayin' look

00:51:42.799 --> 00:51:45.541
I'm not the big I am,
I'm not a big extrovert,

00:51:45.541 --> 00:51:47.227
but I think I've got a compelling idea

00:51:47.227 --> 00:51:48.791
that's worth listening to.

00:51:48.791 --> 00:51:52.628
If they said that to me, my
heart would go out to them

00:51:52.628 --> 00:51:56.795
and emotionally I'd feel
tremendous resonance and connection

00:51:58.635 --> 00:52:01.640
so I don't think entrepreneurs

00:52:01.640 --> 00:52:04.640
are stereotypical, clone extroverts,

00:52:05.907 --> 00:52:07.961
I think there's so many
different types of entrepreneurs

00:52:07.961 --> 00:52:11.815
and that's the richness of
diversity that we celebrate.

00:52:11.815 --> 00:52:13.415
I just think part of that

00:52:13.415 --> 00:52:16.888
is genuine honesty and authenticity

00:52:16.888 --> 00:52:19.043
about acceptin' who
you are and just sayin'

00:52:19.043 --> 00:52:21.670
I'm not going to change
that 'cause that's my soul.

00:52:21.670 --> 00:52:23.884
But I've got a brilliant
idea and it's going to change

00:52:23.884 --> 00:52:26.934
your world and I want to run it past you.

00:52:26.934 --> 00:52:28.612
- I like it, I like it.

00:52:28.612 --> 00:52:32.202
The heart, the Stephen Kelly heart.

00:52:32.202 --> 00:52:34.202
Next question coming in,

